{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019iczj9WAgcaXzby2mmupQn","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages\" Korean premium\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.15425v1\"},{\"title\":\"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/74bb24dca8334adce292883b4b651eda-Abstract.html\"},{\"title\":\"Language model tokenizers introduce unfairness between languages\",\"url\":\"https://intelligent-earth.ox.ac.uk/node/4027061\"},{\"title\":\"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages\",\"url\":\"https://icml.cc/virtual/2023/28966\"},{\"title\":\"neurips.cc\",\"url\":\"https://neurips.cc/virtual/2023/poster/72721\"},{\"title\":\"tokenization fairness\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/AleksandarPetrov/tokenization-fairness\"},{\"title\":\"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages\",\"url\":\"https://liner.com/review/language-model-tokenizers-introduce-unfairness-between-languages\"},{\"title\":\"language model tokenizers introduce 1\",\"url\":\"https://sotaverified.org/papers/language-model-tokenizers-introduce-1\"},{\"title\":\"language model tokenizers introduce unfairness between languages  40c63e9e\",\"url\":\"https://object.cloud.sdsc.edu/v1/AUTH_da4962d3368042ac8337e2dfdd3e7bf3/ml-papers-txt/NeurIPS/2023/language_model_tokenizers_introduce_unfairness_between_languages__40c63e9e.txt\"}]\n\nThe search found the paper, but nothing in the results addresses a \"Korean premium.\" The results cover the paper's general findings, not a Korean-specific figure.\n\nThe paper is by Aleksandar Petrov, Emanuele La Malfa, Philip Torr, and Adel Bibi, and it was published at NeurIPS 2023. Its core claim is that unequal treatment of languages starts at tokenization, before a model runs. The same text translated into different languages can have drastically different tokenization lengths, with differences up to 15 times in some cases. The authors also found that these disparities persist even for tokenizers that are intentionally trained for multilingual support.\n\nThe practical consequences are cost, speed, and context limits. According to the abstract, this induces unfair treatment for some language communities in regard to the cost of accessing commercial language services, the processing time and latency, as well as the amount of content that can be provided as context to the models. The authors conclude that we should train future language models using multilingually fair subword tokenizers.\n\nSources for the paper include the NeurIPS proceedings page, the arXiv preprint, the Oxford Intelligent Earth listing, and the authors' GitHub repository, which has the experiment code and a project page.\n\nIf you want the Korean-specific numbers behind the \"premium,\" I can run a follow-up search aimed at the paper's full text or its per-language tables.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8cf2953f-a072-4c71-af37-24387fe02203","uuid":"07fa5e89-a592-446e-86b1-a892438aac35","timestamp":"2026-10-18T11:10:35.296Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"\"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages\" Korean premium","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01GKJgj1VM6iK9VXh5ztRVKy","content":[{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2305.15425v1"},{"title":"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages","url":"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2023/hash/74bb24dca8334adce292883b4b651eda-Abstract.html"},{"title":"Language model tokenizers introduce unfairness between languages","url":"https://intelligent-earth.ox.ac.uk/node/4027061"},{"title":"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages","url":"https://icml.cc/virtual/2023/28966"},{"title":"neurips.cc","url":"https://neurips.cc/virtual/2023/poster/72721"},{"title":"tokenization fairness","url":"https://github.com/AleksandarPetrov/tokenization-fairness"},{"title":"Language Model Tokenizers Introduce Unfairness Between Languages","url":"https://liner.com/review/language-model-tokenizers-introduce-unfairness-between-languages"},{"title":"language model tokenizers introduce 1","url":"https://sotaverified.org/papers/language-model-tokenizers-introduce-1"},{"title":"language model tokenizers introduce unfairness between languages  40c63e9e","url":"https://object.cloud.sdsc.edu/v1/AUTH_da4962d3368042ac8337e2dfdd3e7bf3/ml-papers-txt/NeurIPS/2023/language_model_tokenizers_introduce_unfairness_between_languages__40c63e9e.txt"}]},"The search found the paper, but nothing in the results addresses a \"Korean premium.\" The results cover the paper's general findings, not a Korean-specific figure.\n\nThe paper is by Aleksandar Petrov, Emanuele La Malfa, Philip Torr, and Adel Bibi, and it was published at NeurIPS 2023. Its core claim is that unequal treatment of languages starts at tokenization, before a model runs. The same text translated into different languages can have drastically different tokenization lengths, with differences up to 15 times in some cases. The authors also found that these disparities persist even for tokenizers that are intentionally trained for multilingual support.\n\nThe practical consequences are cost, speed, and context limits. According to the abstract, this induces unfair treatment for some language communities in regard to the cost of accessing commercial language services, the processing time and latency, as well as the amount of content that can be provided as context to the models. The authors conclude that we should train future language models using multilingually fair subword tokenizers.\n\nSources for the paper include the NeurIPS proceedings page, the arXiv preprint, the Oxford Intelligent Earth listing, and the authors' GitHub repository, which has the experiment code and a project page.\n\nIf you want the Korean-specific numbers behind the \"premium,\" I can run a follow-up search aimed at the paper's full text or its per-language tables."],"durationSeconds":6.0722417,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019iczj9WAgcaXzby2mmupQn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018n1NFMdMnpsq46ECrmkMwv","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Korean vs English token count ratio GPT-4 cl100k o200k tokenizer\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Korean Penalty of Large Language Models Derived by the Tokenizer ICICPE 2024\",\"url\":\"https://hiai.korea.ac.kr/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Korean-Penalty-of-Large-Language-Models-Derived-by-the-Tokenizer_ICICPE_2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"The Non-English Token Tax — Korean Costs 1.4x, Measured\",\"url\":\"https://jangwook.net/en/blog/en/multilingual-llm-token-tax-experiment/\"},{\"title\":\"DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/claudeguide/korean-vs-english-do-you-pay-more-tokens-for-the-same-prompt-jm8\"},{\"title\":\"Escrever prompt em português gasta mais token que em inglês?\",\"url\":\"https://horadecodar.com.br/?p=43288\"},{\"title\":\"# Need more efficient tokenizer for Korean\",\"url\":\"https://community.openai.com/t/need-more-efficient-tokenizer-for-korean/286682.md\"},{\"title\":\"Need more efficient tokenizer for Korean\",\"url\":\"https://community.openai.com/t/need-more-efficient-tokenizer-for-korean/286682\"},{\"title\":\"token counting\",\"url\":\"https://aischool.lillytechsystems.com/tiktokenizer/token-counting.html\"},{\"title\":\"By Christian Prokopp on 2024-04-12\",\"url\":\"https://www.bolddata.org/blog/how-many-words-128k-tokens\"},{\"title\":\"tokenization visualizer\",\"url\":\"https://blakecrosley.com/blog/tokenization-visualizer\"}]\n\nThe sources give a wide range, roughly 1.3x to nearly 4x, and most measure only one tokenizer or a small sample.\n\n**Measured ratios with o200k_base:** A developer measured 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with o200k_base and got a mean Korean-to-English ratio of 1.30x, with a median of 1.33x and a range of 1.09x to 1.42x. Technical paragraphs were the worst case at 1.42x. Measured across 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with the o200k_base BPE tokenizer, the mean ratio was 1.30x (median 1.33x, range 1.09x to 1.42x). A separate blog that tokenized 285 multilingual article pairs reported Korean at 1.38x English tokens and Japanese at 1.34x, also using o200k_base among other tokenizers. Korean ran 1.38x English tokens, Japanese 1.34x.\n\n**Higher estimates:** Another blog reports every Korean translation costs approximately 2.8x the English equivalent, but the post doesn't clearly specify which tokenizer or method was used. An academic paper from Korea University, which used GPT-4's tiktoken tokenizer (cl100k-family) on the FLORES-200 corpus, found that tokenization of the Korean sentence resulted in nearly four times more tokens in one example. That paper's aggregate ratio is what matters for your question, and I did not retrieve it.\n\n**cl100k vs o200k:** The o200k_base vocabulary is larger. One source notes that o200k_base doubled the vocabulary relative to cl100k_base of GPT-4, going from 100 thousand to 200 thousand tokens. In a forum example, the same Korean text produced about 103 tokens under cl100k_base, over 2 times of the previous one, which is the tokenizer that produced the lower count. This suggests o200k reduces the Korean penalty compared with cl100k, but I did not find a head-to-head cl100k vs o200k ratio on the same corpus.\n\n**Caveats:** The sample sizes are small, and the results depend on text type and tokenizer version. One rough reference table lists Korean at about 2–4 characters per token, but it doesn't say whether those figures were measured. The most reliable comparison I found is the 285-pair o200k study, but I could not find a published cl100k number from the same dataset. For a definitive ratio, run tiktoken with both `cl100k_base` and `o200k_base` on your own parallel corpus (e.g., FLORES-200).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8cf2953f-a072-4c71-af37-24387fe02203","uuid":"2476c56d-1ee8-4230-9877-1626f43f3b7b","timestamp":"2026-10-18T11:10:36.735Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Korean vs English token count ratio GPT-4 cl100k o200k tokenizer","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01EXGYYcpvRw6TwYvhCa5Jr9","content":[{"title":"Korean Penalty of Large Language Models Derived by the Tokenizer ICICPE 2024","url":"https://hiai.korea.ac.kr/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Korean-Penalty-of-Large-Language-Models-Derived-by-the-Tokenizer_ICICPE_2024.pdf"},{"title":"The Non-English Token Tax — Korean Costs 1.4x, Measured","url":"https://jangwook.net/en/blog/en/multilingual-llm-token-tax-experiment/"},{"title":"DEV Community","url":"https://dev.to/claudeguide/korean-vs-english-do-you-pay-more-tokens-for-the-same-prompt-jm8"},{"title":"Escrever prompt em português gasta mais token que em inglês?","url":"https://horadecodar.com.br/?p=43288"},{"title":"# Need more efficient tokenizer for Korean","url":"https://community.openai.com/t/need-more-efficient-tokenizer-for-korean/286682.md"},{"title":"Need more efficient tokenizer for Korean","url":"https://community.openai.com/t/need-more-efficient-tokenizer-for-korean/286682"},{"title":"token counting","url":"https://aischool.lillytechsystems.com/tiktokenizer/token-counting.html"},{"title":"By Christian Prokopp on 2024-04-12","url":"https://www.bolddata.org/blog/how-many-words-128k-tokens"},{"title":"tokenization visualizer","url":"https://blakecrosley.com/blog/tokenization-visualizer"}]},"The sources give a wide range, roughly 1.3x to nearly 4x, and most measure only one tokenizer or a small sample.\n\n**Measured ratios with o200k_base:** A developer measured 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with o200k_base and got a mean Korean-to-English ratio of 1.30x, with a median of 1.33x and a range of 1.09x to 1.42x. Technical paragraphs were the worst case at 1.42x. Measured across 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with the o200k_base BPE tokenizer, the mean ratio was 1.30x (median 1.33x, range 1.09x to 1.42x). A separate blog that tokenized 285 multilingual article pairs reported Korean at 1.38x English tokens and Japanese at 1.34x, also using o200k_base among other tokenizers. Korean ran 1.38x English tokens, Japanese 1.34x.\n\n**Higher estimates:** Another blog reports every Korean translation costs approximately 2.8x the English equivalent, but the post doesn't clearly specify which tokenizer or method was used. An academic paper from Korea University, which used GPT-4's tiktoken tokenizer (cl100k-family) on the FLORES-200 corpus, found that tokenization of the Korean sentence resulted in nearly four times more tokens in one example. That paper's aggregate ratio is what matters for your question, and I did not retrieve it.\n\n**cl100k vs o200k:** The o200k_base vocabulary is larger. One source notes that o200k_base doubled the vocabulary relative to cl100k_base of GPT-4, going from 100 thousand to 200 thousand tokens. In a forum example, the same Korean text produced about 103 tokens under cl100k_base, over 2 times of the previous one, which is the tokenizer that produced the lower count. This suggests o200k reduces the Korean penalty compared with cl100k, but I did not find a head-to-head cl100k vs o200k ratio on the same corpus.\n\n**Caveats:** The sample sizes are small, and the results depend on text type and tokenizer version. One rough reference table lists Korean at about 2–4 characters per token, but it doesn't say whether those figures were measured. The most reliable comparison I found is the 285-pair o200k study, but I could not find a published cl100k number from the same dataset. For a definitive ratio, run tiktoken with both `cl100k_base` and `o200k_base` on your own parallel corpus (e.g., FLORES-200)."],"durationSeconds":8.290796400000001,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_018n1NFMdMnpsq46ECrmkMwv","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y7rZVngXjsZSaCx97ME3St","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Claude tokenizer Korean tokens compared to English ratio\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"RedWhale: An Adapted Korean LLM Through Efficient Continual Pretraining\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.11294\"},{\"title\":\"DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/claudeguide/korean-vs-english-do-you-pay-more-tokens-for-the-same-prompt-jm8\"},{\"title\":\"which llm is cheapest for your language tokenizer costs measured 1k5m\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/synthorai/which-llm-is-cheapest-for-your-language-tokenizer-costs-measured-1k5m\"},{\"title\":\"The Non-English Token Tax — Korean Costs 1.4x, Measured\",\"url\":\"https://jangwook.net/en/blog/en/multilingual-llm-token-tax-experiment/\"},{\"title\":\"26401 feature request non english users korean japanese cjk face structural disadvanta\",\"url\":\"https://claudeissues.com/issue/26401-feature-request-non-english-users-korean-japanese-cjk-face-structural-disadvanta\"},{\"title\":\"Korean Penalty of Large Language Models Derived by the Tokenizer ICICPE 2024\",\"url\":\"https://hiai.korea.ac.kr/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Korean-Penalty-of-Large-Language-Models-Derived-by-the-Tokenizer_ICICPE_2024.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"tokenizer blindspots production llm\",\"url\":\"https://tianpan.co/blog/2026/04/20/tokenizer-blindspots-production-llm\"},{\"title\":\"the mystery of the claude 3 tokenizer\",\"url\":\"https://tokencontributions.substack.com/p/the-mystery-of-the-claude-3-tokenizer\"},{\"title\":\"Björn Plüster\",\"url\":\"https://de.linkedin.com/in/bj%C3%B6rn\"}]\n\nI couldn't find an official Anthropic figure for the Korean-to-English token ratio. The estimates I found come from third-party measurements, and they vary widely depending on the tokenizer and method.\n\n- **About 1.3x (not Claude-specific):** One measurement of 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs used OpenAI's o200k_base tokenizer, not Claude's. It found a mean of 1.30x, with a range of 1.09x to 1.42x. The same author argues the ratio is roughly tokenizer-stable, but that is a claim, not a tested result. The same content in Korean uses about 1.3x the tokens of equivalent English. Measured across 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with the o200k_base BPE tokenizer, the mean ratio was 1.30x.\n- **About 1.4x (Claude tokenizer included):** A blog post that loaded Claude's tokenizer from Hugging Face counted tokens across 285 articles translated into four languages. Its headline figure is that Korean costs 1.4x English. I could not see the full Claude-specific totals in the excerpt, so treat this as a secondary source.\n- **1.5x to 3x (anecdotal):** A GitHub feature request on Claude Code says the same meaning expressed in Korean requires approximately 1.5x to 3x more tokens than English. Its examples are approximate, hand-counted phrases, so this is weaker evidence.\n- **Per-character figures:** One analysis reports that on Claude, Korean 106, Japanese 94, against 32 for English net tokens per 100 characters. These are per-character counts, not per-meaning ratios, so they can't be read directly as a language penalty. The same source notes that CJK looks like a 3x tax when read this way, but that reading is misleading.\n- **Older Claude tokenizer:** A 2024-era analysis of Claude's tokenizer reported that Claude's new tokenizer uses 59% more tokens for the same amount of text in English and more than 100% more for German text when compared to OpenAI. That result covers German and English, not Korean. The same post's newest-model claims also mention model names I couldn't verify, so I'd discount them.\n\n**Bottom line:** Most measurements put Korean at roughly 1.3x to 1.5x the tokens of equivalent English, with some anecdotal reports higher. The exact number depends on the text type and tokenizer. For a precise figure for your own content, count tokens with Anthropic's official token counting tools or the Claude tokenizer on your actual text.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8cf2953f-a072-4c71-af37-24387fe02203","uuid":"6ff6cde3-113e-435c-bb68-74201aac4867","timestamp":"2026-10-18T11:10:37.573Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Claude tokenizer Korean tokens compared to English ratio","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01JW5pW2rhaz4NjUiv3mxU4M","content":[{"title":"RedWhale: An Adapted Korean LLM Through Efficient Continual Pretraining","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2408.11294"},{"title":"DEV Community","url":"https://dev.to/claudeguide/korean-vs-english-do-you-pay-more-tokens-for-the-same-prompt-jm8"},{"title":"which llm is cheapest for your language tokenizer costs measured 1k5m","url":"https://dev.to/synthorai/which-llm-is-cheapest-for-your-language-tokenizer-costs-measured-1k5m"},{"title":"The Non-English Token Tax — Korean Costs 1.4x, Measured","url":"https://jangwook.net/en/blog/en/multilingual-llm-token-tax-experiment/"},{"title":"26401 feature request non english users korean japanese cjk face structural disadvanta","url":"https://claudeissues.com/issue/26401-feature-request-non-english-users-korean-japanese-cjk-face-structural-disadvanta"},{"title":"Korean Penalty of Large Language Models Derived by the Tokenizer ICICPE 2024","url":"https://hiai.korea.ac.kr/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/Korean-Penalty-of-Large-Language-Models-Derived-by-the-Tokenizer_ICICPE_2024.pdf"},{"title":"tokenizer blindspots production llm","url":"https://tianpan.co/blog/2026/04/20/tokenizer-blindspots-production-llm"},{"title":"the mystery of the claude 3 tokenizer","url":"https://tokencontributions.substack.com/p/the-mystery-of-the-claude-3-tokenizer"},{"title":"Björn Plüster","url":"https://de.linkedin.com/in/bj%C3%B6rn"}]},"I couldn't find an official Anthropic figure for the Korean-to-English token ratio. The estimates I found come from third-party measurements, and they vary widely depending on the tokenizer and method.\n\n- **About 1.3x (not Claude-specific):** One measurement of 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs used OpenAI's o200k_base tokenizer, not Claude's. It found a mean of 1.30x, with a range of 1.09x to 1.42x. The same author argues the ratio is roughly tokenizer-stable, but that is a claim, not a tested result. The same content in Korean uses about 1.3x the tokens of equivalent English. Measured across 8 meaning-matched Korean/English pairs with the o200k_base BPE tokenizer, the mean ratio was 1.30x.\n- **About 1.4x (Claude tokenizer included):** A blog post that loaded Claude's tokenizer from Hugging Face counted tokens across 285 articles translated into four languages. Its headline figure is that Korean costs 1.4x English. I could not see the full Claude-specific totals in the excerpt, so treat this as a secondary source.\n- **1.5x to 3x (anecdotal):** A GitHub feature request on Claude Code says the same meaning expressed in Korean requires approximately 1.5x to 3x more tokens than English. Its examples are approximate, hand-counted phrases, so this is weaker evidence.\n- **Per-character figures:** One analysis reports that on Claude, Korean 106, Japanese 94, against 32 for English net tokens per 100 characters. These are per-character counts, not per-meaning ratios, so they can't be read directly as a language penalty. The same source notes that CJK looks like a 3x tax when read this way, but that reading is misleading.\n- **Older Claude tokenizer:** A 2024-era analysis of Claude's tokenizer reported that Claude's new tokenizer uses 59% more tokens for the same amount of text in English and more than 100% more for German text when compared to OpenAI. That result covers German and English, not Korean. The same post's newest-model claims also mention model names I couldn't verify, so I'd discount them.\n\n**Bottom line:** Most measurements put Korean at roughly 1.3x to 1.5x the tokens of equivalent English, with some anecdotal reports higher. The exact number depends on the text type and tokenizer. For a precise figure for your own content, count tokens with Anthropic's official token counting tools or the Claude tokenizer on your actual text."],"durationSeconds":8.6320387,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Y7rZVngXjsZSaCx97ME3St","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
